  "solution": {
    "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math> do not form solid bicarbonate. But react with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> to give nitrides. Both of them have similar properties due to diagonal relationship.<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Li</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>&#10230;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi></mover><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mg</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>&#10230;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi></mover><msub><mi>Mg</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>",
